const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"The Architecture of Urban Microclimates\",\"subtitle\":\"Mitigating the Urban Heat Island Effect through Sustainable Civic Design\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nIn contemporary metropolises across the globe, ambient summer temperatures within inner-city precincts routinely exceed those of surrounding rural landscapes by three to seven degrees Celsius. This meteorological disparity is termed the Urban Heat Island (UHI) effect. The underlying physics stems from the massive thermal mass of anthropogenic construction materials. Conventional dense concrete, asphalt road networks, and dark clay brickwork possess high thermal admittance and low surface reflectivity (albedo), absorbing solar radiation voraciously during peak daylight hours and re-radiating thermal energy slowly into the nocturnal boundary layer.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nCompounding this thermal trapping is the geometry of modern high-density architecture. The configuration of towering skyscrapers flanking narrow thoroughfares creates what urban climatologists call 'urban street canyons'. These vertical surfaces obstruct the sky view factor, curtailing convective cooling by inhibiting natural wind circulation and preventing trapped infrared radiation from escaping upward into the atmosphere. Furthermore, anthropogenic waste heat generated by automotive internal combustion engines, domestic split-system air-conditioning units, and subterranean subway ventilation shafts continually pumps megawatts of supplemental heat directly into the streetscape.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nTo reverse these localized heat traps, progressive municipal authorities are deploying passive microclimate design interventions. Chief among these is the strategic enhancement of urban surface albedo. Applying specialized elastomeric cool-roof coatings—which incorporate microscopic ceramic spheres engineered to reflect up to eighty percent of solar irradiance—can diminish rooftop surface temperatures by up to thirty degrees Celsius compared to conventional black bitumen. Simultaneously, porous and permeable interlocking concrete pavers allow subsurface moisture evaporation, providing evaporative cooling that dampens ambient air temperature around pedestrian zones.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nVegetative biosystems offer an equally powerful cooling countermeasure. Through the biological process of evapotranspiration, mature urban shade trees absorb vast quantities of subterranean water through root networks and transpire vapor through leaf stomata, consuming solar energy as latent heat rather than sensible heat. Empirical field studies conducted in Singapore and Stuttgart indicate that a contiguous urban canopy cover of thirty percent can lower localized ambient street-level temperatures by two point five degrees. Extensive green roofs and living vertical facades also act as protective insulating blankets, drastically curtailing building thermal transmission while simultaneously absorbing stormwater runoff.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nFinally, modern aerodynamic building layout can restore natural convective ventilation corridors. By aligning primary urban boulevards parallel to prevailing seasonal breeze vectors and implementing stepped building podiums, civic planners can channel refreshing oceanic or mountain katabatic winds deep into stagnant downtown cores. Computer fluid dynamic (CFD) modeling now allows architects to eliminate localized wind turbulence at ground level while maximizing horizontal air exchange, ensuring that future cities remain habitable in the face of ongoing global climatic warming.\",\"wordCount\":820,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–7 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a5-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"environment\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Urban areas can be up to seven degrees Celsius warmer than neighboring rural areas in summer.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states that ambient summer temperatures in inner-city precincts routinely exceed surrounding rural landscapes by three to seven degrees Celsius.\",\"evidence\":\"ambient summer temperatures within inner-city precincts routinely exceed those of surrounding rural landscapes by three to seven degrees Celsius.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"physics\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Materials with low albedo absorb less solar energy than high-albedo materials.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A explains that materials with low surface reflectivity (albedo) absorb solar radiation voraciously.\",\"evidence\":\"materials possess high thermal admittance and low surface reflectivity (albedo), absorbing solar radiation voraciously\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"architecture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Urban street canyons enhance natural wind circulation at ground level.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that urban street canyons curtail convective cooling by inhibiting natural wind circulation.\",\"evidence\":\"These vertical surfaces obstruct the sky view factor, curtailing convective cooling by inhibiting natural wind circulation\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"urban planning\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Subway ventilation shafts emit more waste heat than private automobile engines.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B mentions waste heat from automobile engines, split-system air-conditioning, and subway shafts together, but makes no comparison regarding which produces more heat.\",\"evidence\":\"anthropogenic waste heat generated by automotive internal combustion engines, domestic split-system air-conditioning units, and subterranean subway ventilation shafts continually pumps megawatts of supplemental heat\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"materials\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Elastomeric cool-roof coatings can reflect up to eighty percent of solar irradiance.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C explicitly notes cool-roof coatings 'engineered to reflect up to eighty percent of solar irradiance.'\",\"evidence\":\"specialized elastomeric cool-roof coatings—which incorporate microscopic ceramic spheres engineered to reflect up to eighty percent of solar irradiance\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Singapore's municipal government mandates green roofs on all newly built commercial towers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D mentions empirical field studies conducted in Singapore and Stuttgart regarding canopy cover, but does not state that green roofs are mandatory on all new commercial towers.\",\"evidence\":\"Empirical field studies conducted in Singapore and Stuttgart indicate that a contiguous urban canopy cover of thirty percent can lower localized ambient street-level temperatures\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"architecture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Stepped building podiums help direct prevailing breezes into stagnant city centers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E confirms that aligning boulevards and implementing stepped building podiums allows planners to channel breezes deep into downtown cores.\",\"evidence\":\"aligning primary urban boulevards parallel to prevailing seasonal breeze vectors and implementing stepped building podiums, civic planners can channel refreshing oceanic or mountain katabatic winds deep into stagnant downtown cores.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 8–13 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"sentence_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a5-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"meteorology\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"The release of stored thermal energy from buildings during the night warms the ____ layer.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"nocturnal boundary\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states thermal energy is re-radiated slowly into the 'nocturnal boundary' layer.\",\"evidence\":\"re-radiating thermal energy slowly into the nocturnal boundary layer.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"architecture\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Tall skyscrapers on both sides of narrow streets form formations referred to as ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"urban street canyons\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that this architectural configuration creates what climatologists call 'urban street canyons'.\",\"evidence\":\"creates what urban climatologists call 'urban street canyons'.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"materials\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Cool-roof coatings contain microscopic ____ that bounce away solar radiation.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"ceramic spheres\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes coatings that incorporate 'microscopic ceramic spheres' engineered to reflect irradiance.\",\"evidence\":\"incorporate microscopic ceramic spheres engineered to reflect up to eighty percent of solar irradiance\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"materials\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Pedestrian walkways can achieve evaporative cooling by installing ____ concrete pavers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"porous and permeable\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes that 'porous and permeable interlocking concrete pavers allow subsurface moisture evaporation'.\",\"evidence\":\"porous and permeable interlocking concrete pavers allow subsurface moisture evaporation\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Through evapotranspiration, solar heat is consumed by trees in the form of ____ heat.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"latent\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states that trees consume solar energy as 'latent heat rather than sensible heat'.\",\"evidence\":\"consuming solar energy as latent heat rather than sensible heat.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"type\":\"sentence_completion\",\"topic\":\"technology\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"Architects rely on computer ____ modeling to anticipate wind behavior before construction.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"fluid dynamic\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to 'Computer fluid dynamic (CFD) modeling' used by architects.\",\"evidence\":\"Computer fluid dynamic (CFD) modeling now allows architects to eliminate localized wind turbulence\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"Deep-Sea Bioluminescence and Photophore Evolution\",\"subtitle\":\"The Biochemical Strategies of Organisms Inhabiting the Ocean Twilight Zone\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nDescending beneath two hundred meters of oceanic depth, sunlight attenuates into a dim, monochromatic indigo twilight before vanishing entirely at one thousand meters into the aphotic midnight bathypelagic abyss. In this immense, high-pressure oceanic biome—representing the largest contiguous living habitat on Earth—more than seventy-five percent of macroscopic marine organisms possess the capacity to generate living light. This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, is not a decorative evolutionary curiosity; it is a primary sensory mechanism essential for survival, hunting, mate communication, and concealment.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nBiochemically, bioluminescence involves an exergonic oxidation reaction catalyzed by specialized proteins. The core substrate is a luciferin molecule, which, when oxidized in the presence of an enzyme termed luciferase and adenosine triphosphate (ATP), produces an unstable excited-state oxyluciferin intermediate. As this molecule relaxes back to its electronic ground state, it releases discrete photons of light. While terrestrial fireflies emit yellow-green radiation, bathypelagic luminescence is overwhelmingly blue-green, centered precisely around wavelengths of four hundred and seventy nanometers. Water molecules absorb longer red and infrared wavelengths within the first few meters of the surface, whereas blue-green light achieves maximum physical transmission through dense seawater.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThe physical organs dedicated to producing and directing this light are known as photophores. Far from rudimentary cellular patches, complex photophores represent sophisticated optoelectronic micro-structures. Histological analyses show they frequently contain parabolic reflective layers lined with microscopic guanine crystals, biochemical light-producing photocyte clusters, and adjustable gelatinous lenses capable of focusing or diffusing the light beam. Certain mesopelagic squids and viperfish even possess pigmented chromatic filters and muscular shutters, granting them dynamic control over beam intensity and focal divergence.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nOne of the most elegant evolutionary applications of photophores is counterillumination camouflage. In the mesopelagic twilight zone, predators lurk in deeper waters scanning upward for the telltale dark silhouettes of prey backlit against downwelling surface daylight. To render themselves invisible, species such as the hatchetfish (Argyropelecus hemigymnus) have evolved dense arrays of ventral photophores running along their bellies. By monitoring downwelling light with dorsal sensory receptors, the fish adjust their ventral bioluminescent emission to match the exact intensity and spectral hue of the downwelling sunlight, seamlessly obliterating their optical silhouette.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nConversely, bioluminescent emissions serve aggressive predatory functions. The deep-sea anglerfish utilizes a modified dorsal fin ray tipped with a glowing esca—a fleshy luminous lure containing millions of symbiotic bioluminescent bacteria belonging to the genus Photobacterium. Suspended directly above formidable needle-sharp jaws, the pulsating lure attracts inquisitive crustaceans and smaller fish directly into the strike zone. Other ambush predators, like the dragonfish (Malacosteus), have evolved a rare secondary red-light bioluminescent system. Because nearly all deep-sea creatures are completely blind to red light, the dragonfish effectively wields an invisible sniper spotlight, illuminating unsuspecting prey without betraying its own presence.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nFinally, bioluminescence functions as a dynamic defensive shield through 'burglar alarm' displays and sacrificial decoys. When seized by a predator, several species of deep-sea jellyfish and siphonophores discharge dazzling, swirling clouds of glowing extracellular mucus into the water column. This explosive flash momentarily blinds the attacker's dark-adapted retinas and illuminates the predator itself, exposing it to larger apex carnivores in the vicinity. Other organisms, including certain species of polychaete worms, can detach glowing body appendages that continue writhing and pulsing for several minutes, misdirecting predators while the animal quietly escapes into the surrounding darkness.\",\"wordCount\":840,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F, in boxes 14–20 on your answer sheet. You may use any letter more than once.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_information\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a5-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"An explanation of why oceanic living light is predominantly blue-green in color.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that blue-green light (470 nm) achieves maximum physical transmission through dense seawater compared to red wavelengths.\",\"evidence\":\"bathypelagic luminescence is overwhelmingly blue-green, centered precisely around wavelengths of four hundred and seventy nanometers. Water molecules absorb longer red and infrared wavelengths\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"A description of a predator using light of a wavelength that its prey cannot detect.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E details the dragonfish using red light, which other deep-sea creatures cannot see.\",\"evidence\":\"Because nearly all deep-sea creatures are completely blind to red light, the dragonfish effectively wields an invisible sniper spotlight\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Details of physical structures that focus and reflect generated light.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes photophores containing parabolic reflective layers lined with guanine crystals and gelatinous lenses.\",\"evidence\":\"parabolic reflective layers lined with microscopic guanine crystals, biochemical light-producing photocyte clusters, and adjustable gelatinous lenses capable of focusing or diffusing the light beam.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"ecology\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"The proportion of larger marine creatures capable of generating light in the deep ocean.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A notes that over seventy-five percent of macroscopic marine organisms possess the capacity to generate living light.\",\"evidence\":\"more than seventy-five percent of macroscopic marine organisms possess the capacity to generate living light.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"evolution\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"How organisms match descending daylight to become invisible to predators beneath them.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D explains counterillumination camouflage where belly photophores match downwelling light.\",\"evidence\":\"adjust their ventral bioluminescent emission to match the exact intensity and spectral hue of the downwelling sunlight, seamlessly obliterating their optical silhouette.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"A defensive strategy where an organism deliberately releases a glowing body part.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"F\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F mentions polychaete worms detaching glowing appendages to misdirect predators.\",\"evidence\":\"certain species of polychaete worms, can detach glowing body appendages that continue writhing and pulsing for several minutes\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"type\":\"matching_information\",\"topic\":\"microbiology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Mention of a symbiotic relationship with bacteria used to generate hunting lures.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E describes the anglerfish lure containing symbiotic bioluminescent bacteria belonging to the genus Photobacterium.\",\"evidence\":\"glowing esca—a fleshy luminous lure containing millions of symbiotic bioluminescent bacteria belonging to the genus Photobacterium.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 21–26 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a5-q21\",\"questionNumber\":21,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"biochemistry\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"The chemical production of bioluminescent light is triggered by an exergonic ____ reaction.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"oxidation\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states that bioluminescence involves an exergonic oxidation reaction.\",\"evidence\":\"bioluminescence involves an exergonic oxidation reaction catalyzed by specialized proteins.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q22\",\"questionNumber\":22,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"biochemistry\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"The base fuel molecule in this reaction is known as a ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"luciferin\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states the core substrate is a luciferin molecule.\",\"evidence\":\"The core substrate is a luciferin molecule\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"histology\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"Photophores often contain reflective linings made from microscopic ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"guanine crystals\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes photophores have reflective layers lined with microscopic guanine crystals.\",\"evidence\":\"parabolic reflective layers lined with microscopic guanine crystals\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Hatchetfish evade bottom predators by utilizing a technique called ____ camouflage.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"counterillumination\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D introduces counterillumination camouflage.\",\"evidence\":\"One of the most elegant evolutionary applications of photophores is counterillumination camouflage.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"The glowing bait of an anglerfish is technically termed an ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"esca\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to the anglerfish's glowing esca.\",\"evidence\":\"modified dorsal fin ray tipped with a glowing esca\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"marine biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Defensive jellyfish can discharge clouds of glowing extracellular ____ to startle predators.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"mucus\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F mentions discharging clouds of glowing extracellular mucus.\",\"evidence\":\"discharge dazzling, swirling clouds of glowing extracellular mucus into the water column.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"The Cognitive Psychology of Metaphor Comprehension\",\"subtitle\":\"From Decorative Rhetoric to the Embodied Foundation of Abstract Thought\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor more than two millennia, the prevailing consensus in Western intellectual tradition relegated metaphor to the periphery of cognition. From Aristotle's Poetics through eighteenth-century Enlightenment rhetoric, metaphors were categorized as stylistic embellishments—decorative linguistic devices employed by poets and persuasive orators to heighten aesthetic impact. Literal language was presumed to be the foundational operating system of human intellect, representing objective truth, whereas figurative expression was dismissed as an imprecise secondary translation. However, in 1980, cognitive linguists George Lakoff and Mark Johnson published Metaphors We Live By, igniting an epistemological shift that fundamentally revolutionized our understanding of human thought.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nLakoff and Johnson formulated Conceptual Metaphor Theory (CMT), arguing that metaphor is not primarily a feature of ornamental language, but an indispensable cognitive architecture that structures how human beings conceptualize reality. According to CMT, abstract concepts such as time, morality, emotional intimacy, and economic systems are understood systematically by mapping structure from tangible, sensorimotor source domains onto abstract target domains. Consider the ubiquitous conceptual metaphor 'TIME IS MONEY'. This underlying cognitive mapping generates countless everyday linguistic expressions: we 'spend' time, 'waste' hours, 'invest' minutes, and run 'out of' time. Crucially, people do not merely speak of time as currency; behavioral experiments prove they schedule, calculate, and value temporal units using the neurological networks that process economic transactions.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThis insight received substantial biological validation with the rise of the embodied cognition paradigm. Embodied cognition asserts that mental operations are grounded in the physical sensorimotor interactions of the human body with its physical environment. When experimental subjects in functional MRI scanners read action-related metaphorical sentences such as 'she had a rough day' or 'he grasped the argument', neuroimaging reveals instantaneous activation in the somatosensory cortex and motor cortex—the exact cerebral regions that process physical tactile roughness and physical manual grasping. The human brain does not discard bodily sensory experience when contemplating abstract ideas; rather, it co-opts physical sensorimotor circuits to simulate abstract meanings.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nSubsequent psycholinguistic research has illuminated the developmental origin of these primary metaphors. Early childhood experiences create neural bindings between concurrent sensorimotor sensations and subjective feelings. During infancy, being physically held close to a caregiver pairs the sensation of physical warmth with the emotional experience of affection, cementing the primary conceptual metaphor 'AFFECTION IS WARMTH'. This subconscious neural binding explains why psychological subjects holding a cup of hot coffee judge a stranger as significantly more trustworthy, compassionate, and interpersonally warm than subjects holding an iced coffee—an empirical demonstration of physical warmth unconsciously priming metaphorical warm social judgments.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nDespite the widespread adoption of CMT, critics have raised important theoretical reservations. Skeptics like psycholinguist Raymond Gibbs and cognitive scientist Gregory Murphy caution against circular reasoning: inferring the existence of conceptual metaphors solely from linguistic patterns, and then using those same linguistic patterns to prove that the mind thinks metaphorically. Furthermore, alternative frameworks such as Career of Metaphor Theory, pioneered by Dedre Gentner, suggest that while novel metaphors require active cross-domain mapping, highly conventional metaphors eventually become lexicalized abstractions that comprehenders process directly as dual-reference category representations without needing sensorimotor simulation.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nNonetheless, the real-world implications of conceptual metaphor research remain profound, particularly within political discourse and public health messaging. When socio-political problems are framed metaphorically as a 'virus' infecting a city, experimental participants overwhelmingly recommend systemic social interventions, such as economic revitalization and community vaccination programs. Conversely, when the identical social issue is framed metaphorically as a 'beast' preying on citizens, participants reflexively endorse punitive, aggressive law enforcement and harsher criminal incarceration. Far from harmless figures of speech, metaphors serve as invisible cognitive templates that subtly constrain how societies interpret problems, assign moral responsibility, and allocate collective resources.\",\"wordCount\":860,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 27–32 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a5-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"linguistics\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Prior to 1980, Western scholars predominantly considered metaphor to be\",\"options\":[\"a decorative stylistic tool used mainly in literature and oratory.\",\"an essential component of abstract reasoning.\",\"an evolutionary holdover from primitive speech.\",\"impossible to study using scientific methods.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states metaphors were categorized as stylistic embellishments—decorative linguistic devices employed by poets and orators.\",\"evidence\":\"metaphors were categorized as stylistic embellishments—decorative linguistic devices employed by poets and persuasive orators to heighten aesthetic impact.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"cognitive science\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"According to Conceptual Metaphor Theory, abstract domains are understood by\",\"options\":[\"translating them into pure mathematical formulas.\",\"mapping structures from physical, sensorimotor experiences.\",\"eliminating emotional terminology from conversation.\",\"memorizing dictionary definitions during adolescence.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that abstract concepts are understood by mapping structure from tangible, sensorimotor source domains onto abstract target domains.\",\"evidence\":\"mapping structure from tangible, sensorimotor source domains onto abstract target domains.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"neuroscience\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"fMRI scans cited in Paragraph C demonstrate that reading phrases like 'he grasped the argument'\",\"options\":[\"causes confusion in the visual processing cortex.\",\"leads to immediate physical fatigue.\",\"triggers activity in brain areas responsible for physical hand movements.\",\"completely bypasses the somatosensory system.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes that reading such phrases activates the motor cortex—the exact cerebral region that processes physical manual grasping.\",\"evidence\":\"neuroimaging reveals instantaneous activation in the somatosensory cortex and motor cortex—the exact cerebral regions that process physical tactile roughness and physical manual grasping.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"The hot coffee experiment described in Paragraph D illustrates that\",\"options\":[\"caffeine enhances social alertness in business meetings.\",\"cold beverages cause psychological hostility.\",\"children learn language faster in warm environments.\",\"physical temperature can unconsciously influence interpersonal evaluation.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D shows holding hot coffee made subjects judge a stranger as more trustworthy and warm, illustrating physical warmth priming metaphorical warmth.\",\"evidence\":\"an empirical demonstration of physical warmth unconsciously priming metaphorical warm social judgments.\"},{\"id\":\"a5-q31\",\"questionNumber\":31,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"linguistics\",\"estimatedBand\":8,\"prompt\":\"Dedre Gentner's Career of Metaphor Theory suggests that frequently used metaphors\",\"options\":[\"turn into lexicalized abstractions processed without bodily simulation.\",\"gradually disappear from everyday vernacular.\",\"become increasingly difficult to comprehend.\",\"are understood faster by non-native language speakers.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E explains Gentner's proposal that highly conventional metaphors become lexicalized abstractions comprehended directly without needing senso